  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Do not let the MSc year end without a tested answer to the research-vs-industry fork, because
    the exam-eligibility window and the industry-experience clock are both already running.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Run yourself through The 4-Checkpoint Protocol above honestly, on paper: daily reality, exam
      proof, money and age window, and reversibility.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then get one real piece of tested evidence before your final semester ends — a serious
      dissertation or research internship if the PhD track is pulling you, or a short internship
      or live project inside a regulatory, QC, bioinformatics, or clinical-research team if the
      industry track is pulling you. Either one gives you real proof to act on, instead of another
      guess.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Whichever fork fits, the degree alone is only the base. The people who build real income
    security from an MSc Biotechnology stack a genuine skill portfolio on top of it — deep,
    demonstrated research proof for the PhD route, or a specific functional specialisation like
    regulatory writing, GMP compliance, or a real bioinformatics pipeline for the industry route —
    instead of assuming the postgraduate label alone will keep paying out. If you want a second,
    structured opinion on which fork fits your specific background, exam attempts, and family
    constraints, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    can help you compare both paths with an actual person, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>
    if you want a clearer read on your working style before you decide.
  </p>

